# Tempus AI: Building a Business Around Sequencing and Precision Medicine

Genomic sequencing has become one of the most important technologies in modern medicine because it allows researchers and healthcare providers to examine a patient's DNA and identify genetic information that can be used to guide diagnosis and treatment. One company that has built its business around sequencing technology is **Tempus AI**. Tempus combines genomic sequencing with artificial intelligence, clinical data, and machine learning to develop more personalized approaches to healthcare.

## Who Founded Tempus?

Tempus was founded in **August 2015 by Eric Lefkofsky**. Lefkofsky created the company after his wife was diagnosed with breast cancer. He wanted to use technology and large amounts of medical data to help doctors make more personalized treatment decisions. The company originally focused heavily on cancer care and has since expanded into areas including cardiology, radiology, neuropsychiatry, and infectious disease.

Tempus's early financing included approximately **$16 million in investments associated with its 2015 business combination**, including $10.5 million from outside investors, $5 million from affiliate investors, and $500,000 from Tempus's CFO.

## What Is Tempus's Main Business Focus?

Tempus's main focus is **precision medicine**. The company combines several types of information, including genomic testing, clinical records, imaging, and other medical data, and uses artificial intelligence to help physicians and researchers make better-informed decisions.

Sequencing is an important part of this model. Tempus uses next-generation sequencing to analyze patients' genetic information, particularly for cancer. The company's technology can help identify mutations and other molecular characteristics that may be relevant to a patient's disease or treatment.

Tempus has essentially created two interconnected businesses. The first is its **diagnostics business**, where it provides genetic and other diagnostic tests to healthcare providers. The second is its **Data and Applications business**, where Tempus uses its large collection of healthcare information to provide data, software, and analytical services to pharmaceutical and biotechnology companies. Tempus reported that its 2025 revenue was approximately **$1.3 billion**, with about $955 million coming from diagnostics and $316 million from Data and Applications.

This creates an interesting business model because the company does not simply make money from performing a genetic test. The testing generates information that can also contribute to a much larger data and technology platform. Tempus reported that its data business grew about 31% in 2025, while its data-licensing business, called Insights, grew about 38%.

## How Does the Business Model Work?

The most interesting part of Tempus's business model is the connection between **sequencing, data, and artificial intelligence**.

A simplified version of the model looks like this:

**Patient → Genetic/clinical testing → Data → AI analysis → Physician/research insights → Pharmaceutical and biotechnology applications**

For example, a patient's tumor can be genetically analyzed. The resulting molecular information can be combined with clinical information and analyzed using Tempus's technology. The company can then provide information to physicians to help with treatment decisions while also developing large datasets that can be useful for pharmaceutical research.

Tempus has therefore created a business in which the sequencing test is only one part of a larger ecosystem. The company can generate revenue from diagnostic testing while also licensing data and providing software and analytical services.

This model demonstrates how sequencing technology can become more than a laboratory technique. It can become the foundation for an entire healthcare technology business.

## What Is Tempus's Current Revenue?

The most recent full-year financial information available shows that Tempus generated approximately **$1.3 billion in revenue during 2025**, an increase of about 83% from 2024. Diagnostics accounted for approximately $955 million, while Data and Applications generated approximately $316 million.

The company also reported **$348.1 million in revenue during the first quarter of 2026**, representing a 36.1% increase compared with the same quarter of the previous year. Tempus subsequently increased its 2026 revenue guidance to approximately **$1.59–$1.60 billion**.

These numbers show that Tempus has moved beyond being an early-stage genomics startup and has developed substantial commercial operations.

## What Does the Future Look Like for Tempus?

Tempus's future is closely connected to the continued development of precision medicine. As sequencing becomes more useful and healthcare systems collect more molecular and clinical information, companies that can organize and analyze these datasets may have opportunities to expand.

One particularly important area is pharmaceutical research. Tempus reported that during 2025 it signed data agreements with more than 70 pharmaceutical and biotechnology customers, including companies such as AstraZeneca, Pfizer, Novartis, Merck, and Eli Lilly. These companies use Tempus's datasets and technology for drug discovery and development.

Tempus is also expanding the types of diseases it works with. Although cancer remains an important part of its business, the company has expanded its technology into hereditary disease, cardiology, radiology, and other areas.

However, future growth is not guaranteed. Tempus operates in a highly competitive healthcare and biotechnology industry, and it must deal with the costs of laboratory testing, regulatory requirements, patient privacy, competition, and the challenge of proving that its technology improves patient care. The company also reported a **$245 million net loss in 2025**, even though revenue grew significantly.

## My Reflection on Tempus's Business Model

I think Tempus is an interesting example of how sequencing technology can be transformed into a business that goes beyond simply selling genetic tests. The company's model connects **genomics, artificial intelligence, healthcare, and pharmaceutical research**.

What stands out to me is that sequencing creates valuable information that can potentially be used multiple times. A genetic test can help an individual patient, while aggregated and appropriately managed data can also contribute to research and drug development. This creates multiple potential sources of revenue from the same technological foundation.

Tempus demonstrates that the future of sequencing may not only be about producing genetic information. The real business opportunity may be in **understanding that information and connecting it with other types of healthcare data**. If Tempus continues to expand its diagnostic testing, AI capabilities, and partnerships with pharmaceutical companies, it could become an important part of the growing precision-medicine industry. At the same time, its continued growth will depend on whether it can translate its technology and data into clinically useful products while maintaining financial sustainability and protecting patient information.

Overall, Tempus provides a good example of how advances in sequencing technology can create an entirely new business model in healthcare—one where genetic information becomes part of a larger ecosystem of diagnostics, artificial intelligence, research, and personalized medicine.
